From d9af51e6f7bbb2353201547ffbdad2c937606932 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: AlexProgrammerDE <40795980+AlexProgrammerDE@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 20:48:28 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] fix(ui): retain Bedrock death screen lifetimes

Update a retained death reason instead of re-entering the screen for each
DeathInfo or visibility-rule update. Keep its existing button deadline and
controls, and do not replace a confirmation or reopen the screen while a
respawn is pending. Adopt the local death timer's already-open screen.
Retire the retained screen when the player becomes alive, its object
changes, or its connection ends. Ignore stale connection updates.

The active OreUI death controller in Bedrock 1.26.60.5/protocol 2225 reads
deathInfo as an observable view property. Its stage effects do not depend
on that text. Executing the original shipped controller confirms that
reason changes retain the button stage and pending respawn timers. Source
gameplay-6de8e340eddced55490b.js SHA-256:
9e7efe423b6cc8a1c1fa92756bd181675cffa51a4728bce386b0081cad1bb979.

Share pending respawn requests between manual screen actions, Java's
automatic null-screen path, and the combat-kill handler. Send once, wait 500 milliseconds, then enter
the loading stage and retry once per second. Retain a pending manual
screen through Java's automatic re-entry and disable both actions.
Leaving the active route, becoming alive, or replacing the connection or
player cancels its timer. Stale player actions cannot start a request.

The combat-kill handler must also retain the immediate-respawn lifetime.
An installed client previously sent another request 50 milliseconds after
the first when a late reason packet entered that handler. Route the event
through the same pending attempt before Java searches the entity index.
The original OreUI controller retains its initial wait through a reason
update in immediate mode, without another command.

Executing the same original controller establishes the initial wait,
retry spacing, route cancellation and reason retention. Late callbacks
schedule their next wait from delivery; do not burst obsolete requests
after a client stall. These controls supply observable hooks, query and
command endpoints, and virtual time. They do not execute native network
actions or the visual component.

Headless regressions exercise real Java death timers and pending command
counts through late text, automatic re-entry, delayed callbacks, active
route changes, and connection/player replacement. The presentation remains
Java. Native OreUI typography, layout, animation, loading presentation,
menu routing and complete lifecycle parity remain unfinished.
